"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"][28]={"university_physics_en:M28-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M28-L1 – Langevin dynamics, the Fokker–Planck equation, and equilibrium<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M28 – Master: Nonequilibrium and many-body physics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M13: statistical ensembles; M26:\nstochastic processes; M17: differential equations · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M27&amp;lesson=L8&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Derive a Fokker–Planck equation\nfrom a Langevin process and distinguish a stationary density from a\nvanishing current.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Microscopic collisions can act like random forces on slow degrees of\nfreedom. Rather than following each collision, we calculate the\nevolution of a probability density\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(x,t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nStationarity initially means only that this density no longer changes;\nin general it is not identical to detailed balance.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>The Ornstein–Uhlenbeck process has linear restoring drift\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>γ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\gamma x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand additive white noise. Here\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W_t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis a Wiener process and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the diffusion parameter. Probability conservation gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mi>J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial_t p=-\\partial_xJ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>γ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J=-\\gamma xp-D\\partial_xp<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nOn an unbounded one-dimensional domain with vanishing boundary current,\nstationarity implies\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>γ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial_x\\ln p=-\\gamma x/D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIntegration and normalization produce the stated Gaussian, with variance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>γ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D/\\gamma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn several dimensions a stationary density can instead support a\ncirculating probability current.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>γ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>D<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>γ<\/mi><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><msubsup><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mi>p<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mfrac><mi>γ<\/mi><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><\/msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>γ<\/mi><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>D<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm dx=-\\gamma x\\,\\mathrm dt+\\sqrt{2D}\\,\\mathrm dW_t,\\quad \\partial_tp=\\gamma\\partial_x(xp)+D\\partial_x^2p,\\quad p_*(x)=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\gamma}{2\\pi D}}\\,e^{-\\gamma x^2/(2D)}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma=2\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=0.5\\,\\mathrm{m}^2/\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe stationary variance is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_*^2=D/\\gamma=0.25\\,\\mathrm{m}^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe standard deviation is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.5\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe plotted Gaussian curves contrast a narrower initial density with the\nstationary density; they are schematic snapshots, not a complete\ntrajectory.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_201_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two normalized Gaussian curves against position; the stationary probability density is wider than the initial density.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two normalized Gaussian curves against\nposition; the stationary probability density is wider than the initial\ndensity.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>The stationary variance is fixed by the ratio\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>γ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D/\\gamma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_*^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=0.8\\,\\mathrm{m}^2/\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma=4\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What additional condition does detailed balance impose on the\ncurrent shown?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\langle x(1\\,\\mathrm s)\\rangle/\\langle x(0)\\rangle<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma=2\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.2\\,\\mathrm{m}^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J(x)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat every position.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.1353<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{-2}\\approx0.1353<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe mean decays exponentially.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>Markov and white-noise assumptions are approximations. With\nstate-dependent diffusion, the drift depends on the Itô or Stratonovich\ninterpretation. The solution uses appropriate boundary conditions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Drift and diffusion determine a stationary distribution. M28-L2\naddresses the kinetic evolution of a phase-space density. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M28&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://davidtong.org/teaching/kinetic-theory/\">David Tong – Kinetic Theory<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Topic reference: kinetics and linear response; Subject relevance to lesson: M28-L1 – Langevin dynamics, the Fokker–Planck equation, and equilibrium · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M28-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M28-L2 – Boltzmann equation, collision operator, and relaxation time<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M28 – Master: Nonequilibrium and many-body physics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M5: kinetic gas theory; M13:\nstatistical physics; M28-L1: transport equation · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M28&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Interpret the terms of the\nBoltzmann equation and solve a relaxation-time approximation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>A dilute gas is described by a phase-space distribution\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒓<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒗<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(\\mathbf r,\\mathbf v,t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFree motion transports this distribution, forces change particle\nvelocities, and collisions redistribute occupancies. This provides a\nconnection between particle dynamics, thermal conductivity, viscosity,\nand other transport phenomena.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>The change in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nalong a classical single-particle trajectory equals the collision\noperator\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C[f]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn a sufficiently dilute gas it can be constructed from binary\ncollisions. A simple relaxation-time approximation uses\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">q<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C[f]\\approx-(f-f_{\\mathrm{eq}})/\\tau<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn a spatially uniform system without external forces,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">q<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">q<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial_t(f-f_{\\mathrm{eq}})=-(f-f_{\\mathrm{eq}})/\\tau<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nif\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">q<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_{\\mathrm{eq}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nremains fixed during relaxation. The result is exponential. A physically\nconsistent collision approximation must preserve particle number,\nmomentum, and energy, typically by choosing an appropriate local\nequilibrium distribution.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mi>f<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒗<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><msub><mi>∇<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒓<\/mi><\/msub><mi>f<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑭<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>⋅<\/mo><msub><mi>∇<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒗<\/mi><\/msub><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">q<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">q<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial_tf+\\mathbf v\\cdot\\nabla_{\\mathbf r}f+\\frac{\\mathbf F}{m}\\cdot\\nabla_{\\mathbf v}f=C[f],\\qquad f(t)-f_{\\mathrm{eq}}=[f(0)-f_{\\mathrm{eq}}]e^{-t/\\tau}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>If the deviation at a fixed velocity is initially\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.30<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta f(0)=0.30<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau=2\\,\\mathrm{ms}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthen after\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4\\,\\mathrm{ms}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nit becomes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.30<\/mn><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.0406<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta f=0.30e^{-2}\\approx0.0406<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe graph shows the normalized deviation. This is a solution of the\nrelaxation equation, not a full solution of the nonlinear Boltzmann\ncollision integral.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_028_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Exponentially decaying normalized deviation from the equilibrium distribution as a function of time in relaxation-time units.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Exponentially decaying normalized\ndeviation from the equilibrium distribution as a function of time in\nrelaxation-time units.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Within the relaxation-time approximation a deviation falls to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nafter one time\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>τ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which physical processes appear in the three terms on the left side\nof the equation?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Compute\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>τ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi>δ<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta f(\\tau)/\\delta f(0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why must a collision approximation conserve particle number?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Time evolution, spatial streaming, and force-driven motion in\nvelocity space.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.3679<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{-1}\\approx0.3679<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Ordinary elastic collisions neither create nor destroy\nparticles.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>Molecular chaos, a dilute classical phase, and local binary\ncollisions are assumptions of the ordinary Boltzmann model. Dense gases\nand quantum fluids require modified treatments.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>The collision operator governs relaxation and transport. M28-L3 turns\nto order parameters and phase transitions. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M28&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://davidtong.org/teaching/kinetic-theory/\">David Tong – Kinetic Theory<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Topic reference: kinetics and linear response; Subject relevance to lesson: M28-L2 – Boltzmann equation, collision operator, and relaxation time · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M28-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M28-L3 – Landau–Ginzburg theory and order parameters<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M28 – Master: Nonequilibrium and many-body physics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M13: phase transitions; M26: field\nfunctionals · <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M28&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Find stationary phases of a\nsymmetric\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>φ<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varphi^4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmodel and explain the role of gradients.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Near a phase transition, many-body physics can often be characterized\nby an order parameter\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>φ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varphi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn a ferromagnet it may describe magnetization along a selected\ndirection. The two signs of this parameter distinguish possible ordered\nphases.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>If the system is invariant under\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ<\/mi><mo>↦<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>φ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varphi\\mapsto-\\varphi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwithout an external field, the local free energy contains only even\npowers. Positive\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nkeeps the potential bounded below. Stationary values obey\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>φ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mi>φ<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><msup><mi>φ<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial f/\\partial\\varphi=a\\varphi+b\\varphi^3-h=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe minimum lies at zero. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a&lt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nzero becomes unstable and minima occur at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>φ<\/mi><mo>±<\/mo><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>±<\/mi><msqrt><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varphi_\\pm=\\pm\\sqrt{-a/b}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe gradient term assigns an energy cost to spatial changes. Hence an\ninterface between regions of opposite order parameter costs energy. This\nfunctional form is the starting point for fluctuation theory.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>φ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo>∫<\/mo><mspace width=\"-0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msup><mi>x<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mfrac><mi>K<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>φ<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mi>a<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><msup><mi>φ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mi>b<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mfrac><msup><mi>φ<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mi>φ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>φ<\/mi><mo>±<\/mo><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>±<\/mi><msqrt><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F[\\varphi]=\\int\\!\\mathrm d^dx\\,\\left[\\frac{K}{2}|\\nabla\\varphi|^2+\\frac{a}{2}\\varphi^2+\\frac{b}{4}\\varphi^4-h\\varphi\\right],\\qquad \\varphi_\\pm=\\pm\\sqrt{-a/b}\\ (a&lt;0,h=0).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>Choose dimensionless parameters\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=-2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe two minima lie at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>±<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varphi=\\pm1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe second derivative\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mrow><mi>′<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>b<\/mi><msup><mi>φ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f^{\\prime\\prime}=a+3b\\varphi^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthere, but\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-2&lt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat the origin. The double-well graphic shows that a stationary-point\ncondition alone does not establish stability.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_141_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Symmetric double-well potential with stable minima at minus one and plus one and an unstable point at the origin.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Symmetric double-well potential with\nstable minima at minus one and plus one and an unstable point at the\norigin.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>A negative quadratic coefficient produces two symmetry-related\nordered states.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find all stationary values of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>φ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varphi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Where are the minima for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=-8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the physical role of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>φ<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K|\\nabla\\varphi|^2/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varphi=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand, for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a&lt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nalso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>±<\/mi><msqrt><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varphi=\\pm\\sqrt{-a/b}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>±<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varphi=\\pm2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It penalizes rapid spatial variation and interfaces.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>Landau theory is a mean-field approximation. Near critical points,\nfluctuations can modify critical exponents and even invalidate a simple\nexpansion. Numerical values in the example are dimensionless.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Symmetry and stability determine the potential. M28-L4 examines how\nsuch theories change with observation scale. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M28&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://davidtong.org/teaching/statistical-field-theory/\">David Tong – Statistical Field Theory<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Topic reference: Landau theory and fluctuations; Subject relevance to lesson: M28-L3 – Landau–Ginzburg theory and order parameters · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M28-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M28-L4 – Scaling, renormalization group, and critical fixed points<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M28 – Master: Nonequilibrium and many-body physics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M28-L3: Landau–Ginzburg theory; M26:\ndimensionless couplings · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M28&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish relevant from\nirrelevant perturbations and derive a correlation-length exponent.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Close to a continuous phase transition, fluctuations appear on many\nlength scales. A renormalization-group (RG) transformation removes\nshort-scale information and describes the remaining physics using\nmodified couplings. Critical systems are characterized by fixed points\nin this coupling space.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>Consider a small dimensionless distance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom a fixed point. Under coarse-graining by a factor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b&gt;1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe linearized perturbation evolves as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>b<\/mi><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><\/msup><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t\\prime=b^{y_t}t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y_t&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nit grows and is called relevant; if\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y_t&lt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nit is irrelevant. Near criticality the correlation length follows\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ξ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>∝<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\xi(t)\\propto |t|^{-\\nu}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nUnder the same coarse-graining, the observed length becomes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ξ<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ξ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\xi\\prime=\\xi/b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nConsistency of these laws requires\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nu=1/y_t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis is a local scaling relation for one relevant variable, not a\nuniversal numerical value applying to all materials.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>b<\/mi><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><\/msup><mi>t<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>ξ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>∝<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>ν<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><\/mfrac><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t\\prime=b^{y_t}t,\\qquad \\xi(t)\\propto|t|^{-\\nu},\\qquad \\nu=\\frac{1}{y_t}\\quad (y_t&gt;0).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>For the illustrative RG map with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y_t=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.02<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=0.02<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbecomes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.04<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t\\prime=0.04<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nafter one step and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.08<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.08<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nafter another. The corresponding exponent is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nu=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe diagram shows a relevant direction in which flow arrows point away\nfrom\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIt is a one-dimensional slice through a generally much larger coupling\nspace.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_248_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Renormalization-group flow with a fixed point at t equals zero and arrows on either side pointing away from it.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Renormalization-group flow with a fixed\npoint at t equals zero and arrows on either side pointing away from\nit.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Relevant couplings grow away from a critical fixed point under\ncoarse-graining.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What does a negative RG eigenvalue mean?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ν<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y_t=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does a small perturbation evolve after two steps with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y_t=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The perturbation shrinks under coarse-graining and is\nirrelevant.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nu=1/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t\\to2t\\to4t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The linearized RG map holds near a fixed point. Marginal variables\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nmultiple relevant couplings, and anisotropic scaling require further\nanalysis.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Fixed points explain universal scaling. M28-L5 studies the response\nto a small time-dependent perturbation. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M28&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://davidtong.org/teaching/statistical-field-theory/\">David Tong – Statistical Field Theory<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Topic reference: Landau theory and fluctuations; Subject relevance to lesson: M28-L4 – Scaling, renormalization group, and critical fixed points · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/sft/sfthtml/S3.html\">David Tong – Renormalisation Group<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Section about RG scaling and critical exponents; Subject relevance to lesson: M28-L4 – Scaling, renormalization group, and critical fixed points · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M28-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M28-L5 – Linear response, the Kubo formula, and causality<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M28 – Master: Nonequilibrium and many-body physics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M10: electrodynamics; M13:\nequilibrium; M27: quantum operators · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M28&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> State a causal susceptibility\nand calculate the real and imaginary parts of a relaxation model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Experiments often measure how a system responds to a weak external\nsignal. If the response is proportional to the perturbation, a\nsusceptibility describes this relation. A physical response cannot\nprecede its cause. Causality is therefore a mathematical constraint on\nthe response function as well as an interpretive principle.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>Let the perturbation of the Hamiltonian be\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H\\prime(t)=-h(t)B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn linear response, the expectation change in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis described by the retarded equilibrium function\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>χ<\/mi><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\chi_{AB}(t)=(i/\\hbar)\\theta(t)\\langle[A(t),B(0)]\\rangle<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA simple phenomenological relaxation model instead uses\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>χ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>χ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\chi(t)=(\\chi_0/\\tau)e^{-t/\\tau}\\theta(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWe explicitly adopt the transform\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>χ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msubsup><mi>χ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\chi(\\omega)=\\int_0^\\infty \\chi(t)e^{i\\omega t}\\,\\mathrm dt<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>χ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>χ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\chi(\\omega)=\\chi_0/(1-i\\omega\\tau)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith real part\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>χ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\chi_0/[1+(\\omega\\tau)^2]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand imaginary part\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>χ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\chi_0\\omega\\tau/[1+(\\omega\\tau)^2]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>t<\/mi><\/msubsup><msub><mi>χ<\/mi><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>χ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>χ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta\\langle A(t)\\rangle=\\int_{-\\infty}^{t}\\chi_{AB}(t-t\\prime)h(t\\prime)\\,\\mathrm dt\\prime,\\qquad \\chi(\\omega)=\\frac{\\chi_0}{1-i\\omega\\tau}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega\\tau=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe values are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Re<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>χ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>χ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Re}\\chi/\\chi_0=1/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Im<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>χ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>χ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Im}\\chi/\\chi_0=1/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBoth plotted curves apply only to this single-relaxation-time model;\nreal materials can combine many relaxation times and resonances. The\nmodel imaginary part reaches its maximum at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega\\tau=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_155_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Real and imaginary parts of Debye susceptibility against dimensionless frequency omega times tau.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Real and imaginary parts of Debye\nsusceptibility against dimensionless frequency omega times\ntau.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>The Debye model has its dissipative maximum at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega\\tau=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What does the step function\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nenforce?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>χ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>χ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\chi(0)/\\chi_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate both components for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega\\tau=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It enforces causality: no response before the perturbation.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Both are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin units of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>χ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\chi_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>Kubo response assumes a sufficiently weak perturbation and suitable\nequilibrium conditions. The sign of the imaginary part depends on\nFourier convention. Debye relaxation is phenomenological rather than a\nuniversal response spectrum.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Linear response connects temporal correlations with measurable\ndissipation. The following lessons apply many-body methods to quantum\nmaterials. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M28&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://davidtong.org/teaching/kinetic-theory/\">David Tong – Kinetic Theory<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Topic reference: kinetics and linear response; Subject relevance to lesson: M28-L5 – Linear response, the Kubo formula, and causality · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M28-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M28-L6 – Weakly interacting bosons and Bogoliubov excitations<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M28 – Master: Nonequilibrium and many-body physics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M12: quantum mechanics; M13: Bose\ndistribution; M27-L8: second quantization · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M28&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate the Bogoliubov\nspectrum and derive its low-momentum sound limit.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>During Bose–Einstein condensation, many bosons can occupy the same\nquantum state. Weak interactions modify the elementary excitations. At\nsmall momentum, collective sound modes appear instead of purely\nfree-particle modes.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>For a homogeneous three-dimensional Bose gas with weak repulsive\ncontact interaction\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand condensate density\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nconsider quadratic fluctuations around a stationary condensate. The free\nenergy of one mode is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msup><mi>k<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon_k=\\hbar^2k^2/(2m)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBogoliubov diagonalization gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>g<\/mi><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_k=\\sqrt{\\varepsilon_k(\\varepsilon_k+2gn_0)}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt small\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≪<\/mo><mi>g<\/mi><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon_k\\ll gn_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand therefore\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≃<\/mo><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_k\\simeq\\hbar ck<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c=\\sqrt{gn_0/m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt high\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe spectrum approaches\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≃<\/mo><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>g<\/mi><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_k\\simeq\\varepsilon_k+gn_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe linear dispersion reflects collective excitations of the condensed\nsystem.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msup><mi>k<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>g<\/mi><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>c<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><mi>m<\/mi><\/mfrac><\/msqrt><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon_k=\\frac{\\hbar^2k^2}{2m},\\qquad E_k=\\sqrt{\\varepsilon_k(\\varepsilon_k+2gn_0)},\\qquad c=\\sqrt{\\frac{gn_0}{m}}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>If the free mode energy is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mi>g<\/mi><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon_k=0.5gn_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>2.5<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>g<\/mi><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.118<\/mn><mi>g<\/mi><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_k=\\sqrt{0.5\\cdot2.5}\\,gn_0\\approx1.118gn_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe graphic plots both energies in units of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">gn_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nagainst normalized wavenumber\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k/k_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mi>/<\/mi><mi>ℏ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k_0=\\sqrt{mgn_0}/\\hbar<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe Bogoliubov branch begins linearly, whereas the free energy depends\nquadratically on momentum.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_026_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Bogoliubov excitation energy and free quadratic dispersion plotted against dimensionless momentum.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Bogoliubov excitation energy and free\nquadratic dispersion plotted against dimensionless\nmomentum.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Interactions replace the free quadratic low-momentum behavior\nwith a linear sound branch.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Derive\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≃<\/mo><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_k\\simeq\\hbar ck<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat small momentum.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>g<\/mi><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_k/(gn_0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nif\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>g<\/mi><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon_k/(gn_0)=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What happens to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><msup><mn>0<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g\\to0^+<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat fixed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>g<\/mi><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><msqrt><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_k\\approx\\sqrt{2gn_0\\varepsilon_k}=\\hbar k\\sqrt{gn_0/m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>3<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.732<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt3\\approx1.732<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\napproaches the free particle energy\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon_k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The model requires weak interactions and small condensate depletion.\nIn lower dimensions, at high temperatures, or under strong correlations,\nthis simple condensate picture can break down.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>The Bogoliubov spectrum describes interacting bosons through\ncollective modes. M28-L7 introduces energy-resolved Green functions. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M28&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-513-many-body-theory-for-condensed-matter-systems-fall-2004/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT OpenCourseWare – Many-Body Theory for Condensed Matter Systems<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lecture outline on many-body methods; not a direct formula verification; Subject relevance to lesson: M28-L6 – Weakly interacting bosons and Bogoliubov excitations · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www2.physik.lmu.de/lehre/vorlesungen/sose_20/TVI_TMP-TA3/index.html\">LMU München – Condensed Matter Many-Body Physics and Field Theory I<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Curriculum on Green functions and quasiparticles; not direct formula verification; Subject relevance to lesson: M28-L6 – Weakly interacting bosons and Bogoliubov excitations · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M28-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M28-L7 – Retarded Green functions and spectral-function normalization<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M28 – Master: Nonequilibrium and many-body physics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M27-L8: second quantization; M28-L5:\ncausality · <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M28&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Normalize the Lorentzian\nspectral density of a canonical fermionic mode and identify its energy\nunits.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Green functions in many-body theory describe the energies at which an\nexcitation can be created or removed. Spectral density makes this\ninformation close to measurable response. Correct normalization requires\ndistinguishing energy\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>E<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom angular frequency\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ω<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand single-particle response from density response.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>Use the energy variable\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>E<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand define\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>π<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Im<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msup><mi>G<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A(E)=-(1/\\pi)\\operatorname{Im}G^R(E)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor exactly one canonical fermionic mode satisfying\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><msup><mi>c<\/mi><mo>†<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{c,c^\\dagger\\}=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn a single-pole model with level energy\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ε<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand width\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Gamma&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nlet\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>G<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>ε<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G^R(E)=[E-\\varepsilon+i\\Gamma]^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIts imaginary part is negative, hence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A(E)\\ge0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSubstituting\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>ε<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=(E-\\varepsilon)/\\Gamma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msubsup><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo>∫<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>u<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty A(E)\\,\\mathrm dE=\\int\\mathrm du/[\\pi(1+u^2)]=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn interacting systems, the retarded self-energy can appear in the Dyson\nform\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>G<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>ε<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Σ<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G^R=[E-\\varepsilon-\\Sigma^R(E)]^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe sum rule rests on canonical operator algebra and correct high-energy\nbehavior; it is not a universal rule for arbitrary correlators.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>G<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>ε<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ<\/mi><mrow><mi>π<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>ε<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msubsup><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G^R(E)=\\frac{1}{E-\\varepsilon+i\\Gamma},\\quad A(E)=\\frac{\\Gamma}{\\pi[(E-\\varepsilon)^2+\\Gamma^2]},\\quad \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} A(E)\\,\\mathrm dE=1.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon=1.0\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Gamma=0.2\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe peak value is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ε<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.592<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A(\\varepsilon)=1/(\\pi\\Gamma)\\approx1.592\\,\\mathrm{eV}^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nEach half-width at half maximum is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Gamma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ngiving a full width of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.4\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe plotted peak has total area one, not unit peak height.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_094_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Lorentzian spectral curve peaking at energy epsilon with a marked full width at half maximum of two Gamma.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Lorentzian spectral curve peaking at\nenergy epsilon with a marked full width at half maximum of two\nGamma.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>In the energy convention, the single-particle spectral density\nhas unit area; its full width at half maximum is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\Gamma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What units does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A(E)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhave if energy is measured in electronvolts?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find the full width at half maximum for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.05<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Gamma=0.05\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is this normalization not automatically valid for a\ndensity–density correlator?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{eV}^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.10\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Its operator algebra and spectral sum rules are different.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The angular-frequency variable\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>ℏ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega=E/\\hbar<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrequires consistent conversion of measure and prefactor. The Lorentzian\nshape assumes constant width and one simple pole. Positivity and unit\narea here refer only to the specified fermionic single-particle\nconvention; other spectral functions can have different signs and\nnormalizations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>The sum rule is a direct physical consistency check. M28-L8\ninterprets a narrow coherent peak as a quasiparticle. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M28&amp;lesson=L8&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-513-many-body-theory-for-condensed-matter-systems-fall-2004/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT OpenCourseWare – Many-Body Theory for Condensed Matter Systems<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lecture outline on many-body methods; not a direct formula verification; Subject relevance to lesson: M28-L7 – Retarded Green functions and spectral-function normalization · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www2.physik.lmu.de/lehre/vorlesungen/sose_20/TVI_TMP-TA3/index.html\">LMU München – Condensed Matter Many-Body Physics and Field Theory I<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Curriculum on Green functions and quasiparticles; not direct formula verification; Subject relevance to lesson: M28-L7 – Retarded Green functions and spectral-function normalization · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M28-L8":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L8\">M28-L8 – Fermi liquids, self-energy, and quasiparticles<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M28 – Master: Nonequilibrium and many-body physics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M13: Fermi gas; M14: condensed\nmatter; M28-L7: spectral function · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M28&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Interpret quasiparticle residue,\nlinewidth, and lifetime using a retarded self-energy.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Electrons in a metal interact, yet often display long-lived\nexcitations near the Fermi surface. Fermi-liquid theory treats these as\nquasiparticles. Their narrow coherent spectral contribution is not the\nwhole single-particle spectrum: interactions redistribute weight to\nother excitations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>Near a simple quasiparticle pole, expand the real self-energy\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Σ<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Sigma^R(k,E)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\naround the Fermi level. At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>F<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=k_F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand energy\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrelative to the chemical potential, the quasiparticle residue is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Re<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Σ<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z=[1-\\partial_E\\operatorname{Re}\\Sigma^R]^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA negative retarded imaginary part represents damping. For weak damping,\ndefine the full energy linewidth\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">q<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mi>Z<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Im<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Σ<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Gamma_{\\mathrm{qp}}=-2Z\\operatorname{Im}\\Sigma^R(k,E_*)&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the usual population lifetime\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">q<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau=\\hbar/\\Gamma_{\\mathrm{qp}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nCompare this with the Lorentzian in M28-L7, where\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Gamma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenoted half the full width. Explicit definitions prevent a\nfactor-of-two error.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mrow><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><\/msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Re<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Σ<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>F<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">q<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mi>Z<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Im<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Σ<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>ℏ<\/mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">q<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z=\\left[1-\\left.\\partial_E\\operatorname{Re}\\Sigma^R(k,E)\\right|_{k_F,0}\\right]^{-1},\\quad \\Gamma_{\\mathrm{qp}}=-2Z\\operatorname{Im}\\Sigma^R(k,E_*),\\quad\\tau=\\frac{\\hbar}{\\Gamma_{\\mathrm{qp}}}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.75<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z=0.75<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe real self-energy derivative at the Fermi level is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>Z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-1/Z=-1/3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor a full linewidth\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">q<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.040<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Gamma_{\\mathrm{qp}}=0.040\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>6.5821<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>16<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hbar\\approx6.5821\\times10^{-16}\\,\\mathrm{eV\\,s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe lifetime is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.65<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>14<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>16.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau\\approx1.65\\times10^{-14}\\,\\mathrm s=16.5\\,\\mathrm{fs}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe diagram combines a narrow coherent peak with a broad incoherent\ncontribution. This is a spectral-weight model, not experimental\ndata.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_092_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Spectral distribution showing a narrow coherent quasiparticle peak and a broad incoherent background component.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Spectral distribution showing a narrow\ncoherent quasiparticle peak and a broad incoherent background\ncomponent.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>The residue\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Z<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmeasures the coherent quasiparticle fraction of the single-particle\nspectral weight.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the real self-energy derivative for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>τ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">q<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.020<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Gamma_{\\mathrm{qp}}=0.020\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>Z<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-Z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrepresent in the simple spectral model?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Zero.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.29<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>14<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>32.9<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3.29\\times10^{-14}\\,\\mathrm s\\approx32.9\\,\\mathrm{fs}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Spectral weight not concentrated in the coherent quasiparticle\npole.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The quasiparticle approximation is a low-energy description. It can\nfail in one-dimensional Luttinger liquids or near certain quantum\ncritical points. Linewidth, residue, and self-energy vary with energy\nand momentum.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Spectral weight and lifetime link many-body self-energy to measurable\nquantum matter. The next module turns to relativistic field and geometry\nconcepts.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-513-many-body-theory-for-condensed-matter-systems-fall-2004/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT OpenCourseWare – Many-Body Theory for Condensed Matter Systems<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lecture outline on many-body methods; not a direct formula verification; Subject relevance to lesson: M28-L8 – Fermi liquids, self-energy, and quasiparticles · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www2.physik.lmu.de/lehre/vorlesungen/sose_20/TVI_TMP-TA3/index.html\">LMU München – Condensed Matter Many-Body Physics and Field Theory I<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Curriculum on Green functions and quasiparticles; not direct formula verification; Subject relevance to lesson: M28-L8 – Fermi liquids, self-energy, and quasiparticles · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
